@preamble use("math/native")
floor
Returns the largest integer less than or equal to x using exact Aiki numbers.

floor(x)

floor(3.7)     # 3
floor(-2.3)    # -3
===
ceil
Returns the smallest integer greater than or equal to x using exact Aiki numbers.

ceil(x)

ceil(3.2)     # 4
ceil(-2.7)    # -2
===
modulo
Returns the Euclidean remainder of a divided by b. The result is non-negative
for a non-zero divisor.

modulo(a, b)

modulo(10, 3)     # 1
modulo(-10, 3)    # 2
===
sin
Returns the sine of x using Taylor series expansion.
The terms parameter bounds the work performed rather than specifying a target
error. For a fixed x, more terms generally improve the approximation, but the
gain depends on the magnitude of x and is not linear. No fixed term count
guarantees a fixed number of correct digits for all inputs.

sin(x, terms)

sin(0, 5)           # 0
sin(3.14159/2, 10)  # approximately 1
===
cos
Returns the cosine of x using Taylor series expansion.
The terms parameter bounds the work performed rather than specifying a target
error. For a fixed x, more terms generally improve the approximation, but the
gain depends on the magnitude of x and is not linear. No fixed term count
guarantees a fixed number of correct digits for all inputs.

cos(x, terms)

cos(0, 5)           # 1
cos(3.14159, 10)    # approximately -1
===
sqrt
Returns the square root of x using Newton's method.
The iterations parameter bounds the work performed rather than specifying a
target error. Newton's method usually gains correct digits rapidly, but the
number of useful iterations depends on x; once the displayed approximation
stops changing at the required precision, further iterations only enlarge the
exact rational used to represent it.

sqrt(x, iterations)

sqrt(4, 10)     # 2
sqrt(2, 10)     # approximately 1.414
